chapter_notes.2B:
Exposure gate: Bangladesh had no internationalized conflict events or fatalities in 2022, so this is a limited-exposure Chapter 2B case. The evidence therefore centers on diplomatic posture, UN voting, peacekeeping, and Rohingya-related conflict management rather than battlefield conduct.

**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0008"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}

**New evidence**
- Bangladesh’s UN statement on the Ukraine emergency special session called for an immediate cessation of hostilities, restraint by all parties, and a return to diplomacy, with safe passage for civilians.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement DPR Ukraine Crisis","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-03-01","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952668535ce18e1c05a9c55","claim":"Bangladesh called for an immediate cessation of hostilities in Ukraine, urged restraint, and asked for a return to diplomacy and dialogue with safe passage for civilians.","locator":"Statement text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government statement at the UN.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly on international peace conduct.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Hasina's government; indirect but relevant to ruler-period conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This is diplomatic posture, not proof of operational restraint in any military campaign."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh voted in favour of the March 24, 2022 Ukraine humanitarian resolution that demanded aid access and civilian protection.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh votes in favour of Ukraine on UN resolution","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-03-24","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/bangladesh-votes-in-favour-of-ukraine-on-un-resolution","claim":"Bangladesh voted in favour of the UN General Assembly resolution demanding aid access and civilian protection in Ukraine.","locator":"Article text on the March 24 UNGA vote","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting quoting the UN vote outcome and Bangladesh's position; indirect but specific.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to the Ukraine crisis.","ruler_attribution":"State foreign-policy choice under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["The vote supports peace and civilian protection, but it does not show broader conflict-management consistency by itself."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- The official UN recorded vote on October 12, 2022 shows Bangladesh voted yes on the Ukraine territorial-integrity resolution.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Territorial integrity of Ukraine: defending the principles of the Charter of the United Nations","publisher":"United Nations Digital Library","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3990400","claim":"Bangladesh voted yes on the General Assembly resolution condemning Russia's attempted annexation of Ukrainian territory.","locator":"Vote summary, vote date 2022-10-12, Bangladesh listed as Y","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN voting record with country-level vote trace.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State vote under Hasina's government; indirect ruler attribution.","contrary_evidence":["A single pro-law vote does not erase the earlier abstention on a related Ukraine resolution."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh abstained on the November 2022 Ukraine reparations resolution, showing a more cautious and less aligned posture on later Ukraine accountability measures.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh abstains from UN vote against Russia","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/world/2022-russian-invasion-ukraine/news/bangladesh-abstains-unga-vote-against-russia-3170221","claim":"Bangladesh abstained from the UN General Assembly resolution calling for Russia to be held accountable for violating international law by invading Ukraine.","locator":"Lead paragraphs and vote explanation","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed local reporting on the specific vote and Bangladesh's position.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State foreign-policy choice under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["This is a contrary data point against a uniformly hard line on Russia.","The article notes Bangladesh had voted differently on earlier Ukraine resolutions."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh explicitly endorsed the International Court of Justice as a channel for peaceful settlement of disputes.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement DPR ICJ report","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/695266a135ce18e1c05aa778","claim":"Bangladesh said it fully supports the ICJ's role in promoting the peaceful settlement of international disputes to maintain international peace and security.","locator":"Statement text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government statement at the UN.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to peace/legal settlement.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["This is an institutional commitment, not proof of any specific negotiation outcome."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh framed the UN Charter and peaceful settlement as central to international peace and security.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement DPR Special Committee on the Charter of the UN","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952668d35ce18e1c05a9f98","claim":"Bangladesh said the UN Charter's role in maintaining international peace and security depends on states refraining from force and respecting sovereignty, territorial integrity, and peaceful settlement of disputes.","locator":"Statement text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government statement at the UN.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to lawful security norms.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["Normative language does not establish implementation in specific crises."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh warned in October 2022 that emerging technologies, including AI and biotechnology, could jeopardize international peace and security.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement Counsellor Thematic Debate: Other Disarmament Measures And International Security","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-10-24","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952668535ce18e1c05a9c5f","claim":"Bangladesh warned that rapid technological advances, including artificial intelligence and biotechnology, could pose risks to international peace and security and require vigilance against malicious use.","locator":"Opening paragraphs of the statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government diplomatic statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to dual-use/catastrophic-risk governance.","ruler_attribution":"State-level position under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["This is broad multilateral language, not a Bangladesh-specific policy implementation record."],"lenses":["2B.6","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh said outer space should be used exclusively for peaceful purposes.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement Counsellor Thematic Debate: Outer Space (disarmament aspects)","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/695266b135ce18e1c05aacdf","claim":"Bangladesh stated that outer space should be used exclusively for peaceful purposes and described access to space as a global common good.","locator":"Statement text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government diplomatic statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to lawful, peaceful security framing.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is aspirational and does not show concrete national capability management."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.10"]}

- Bangladesh told the UN in November 2022 that it had used multilateral diplomacy for safe, voluntary, sustainable Rohingya return and warned the issue could become a regional security crisis.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement DPR Situation Of Rohingya Muslims In Myanmar","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952667135ce18e1c05a9221","claim":"Bangladesh said it had pursued bilateral and multilateral diplomatic efforts for the safe, voluntary, and sustainable return of Rohingya refugees and warned the crisis could become a regional security problem.","locator":"Statement text at the adoption of the Third Committee resolution","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government statement at the UN.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to regional peace and stability.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Hasina's government; direct policy attribution is moderate because it reflects the official line rather than a personal quote.","contrary_evidence":["Bangladesh's strategy still depended on Myanmar conditions and international cooperation, so results were limited."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

- At the March 2022 CSW side event, Bangladesh said it was working with the ICC prosecution team and the OIC to ensure justice for Rohingya women and youth.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Statement HEPR Rohingya Crisis","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-03-21","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952669435ce18e1c05aa2b4","claim":"Bangladesh said it was working with the ICC prosecution team and the OIC to ensure justice for Rohingya women and youth and framed a safe, dignified future for Rohingya as requiring a lasting solution beyond camp containment.","locator":"Statement text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 government statement at the UN.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to peacebuilding and accountability.","ruler_attribution":"State position under Hasina's government.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is humanitarian and justice-oriented, not a concrete bilateral settlement result."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- As of 31 December 2022, Bangladesh was the largest contributor to UN peacekeeping with 7,233 uniformed personnel.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"MINURSO’s Peacekeepers: National Day of Bangladesh","publisher":"United Nations Peacekeeping","publication_date":"2023-03-26","url":"https://peacekeeping.un.org/en/node/122258","claim":"As of 31 December 2022, Bangladesh ranked as the largest contributing country to UN peacekeeping with 7,233 uniformed personnel.","locator":"Opening section on Bangladesh's peacekeeping role","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN peacekeeping reporting, albeit on a 2023 page summarizing 2022 status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly tied to year-end 2022 status.","ruler_attribution":"State institutional posture under Hasina's government, not a personal operational order.","contrary_evidence":["Peacekeeping size does not itself prove wise military spending or superior diplomacy."],"lenses":["2B.8","2B.10"]}

- In Cyprus, UN peacekeeping said Bangladesh and Indonesia were among the largest contributors and had joined the UNPOL component in November 2022.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNFICYP welcomes Bangladesh and Indonesia at UNPOL flag-raising ceremony","publisher":"United Nations Peacekeeping","publication_date":"2022-12-13","url":"https://peacekeeping.un.org/en/node/121672","claim":"UN peacekeeping described Bangladesh as one of the largest contributors to UN peacekeeping and noted that Bangladesh joined the UNPOL component of UNFICYP in November 2022, with an explicit commitment to increasing female peacekeepers.","locator":"Article text and quoted remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN peacekeeping mission page with dated 2022 deployment details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to peaceful international security roles.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional, not personal; still relevant to Hasina-era state conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This shows peacekeeping participation, not a direct ruler-level peace decision in a crisis."],"lenses":["2B.8","2B.10"]}

- Bangladeshi peacekeepers in Mali were credited with protecting civilians and UN personnel in and around Bamako.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN Police honour peacekeepers in Bamako and Goundam","publisher":"United Nations Peacekeeping","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://peacekeeping.un.org/en/node/42013","claim":"Bangladeshi peacekeepers deployed since 14 January 2022 helped protect civilians and UN personnel in and around Bamako.","locator":"Article body describing the Bangladeshi Formed Police Unit","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN peacekeeping mission report on a 2022 deployment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State institutional conduct under Hasina's government, not a personal field command action.","contrary_evidence":["This is peace-support activity, not proof of broader foreign-policy restraint."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- Reuters, via Euronews, reported in late December 2022 that Bangladesh was trying to stop Rohingya refugees from risking their lives at sea.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh tries to stop Rohingya risking their lives at sea - official","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-12-27","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/12/27/asia-rohingya-bangladesh","claim":"Bangladesh was trying to stop Rohingya refugees from risking their lives in boats to Southeast Asia amid fears that 2022 could be one of the deadliest years at sea for the group.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting carried by Euronews; strong but still secondary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to regional stability and refugee management.","ruler_attribution":"Government policy environment under Hasina's administration.","contrary_evidence":["This addresses humanitarian management and deterrence, not a negotiated peace settlement."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across four main families.

- Kept as strongest: official Bangladesh UN mission statements on Ukraine, the ICJ, the Charter Committee, disarmament and outer space, and Rohingya; UN Digital Library vote records; UN Peacekeeping records; and one Reuters-based regional report.
- Kept as meaningful contrary evidence: the March 2022 abstention and the November 2022 abstention on Ukraine reparations.
- Rejected as duplicates: multiple local and regional articles that repeated the same Ukraine vote outcome, and multiple peacekeeping pages that repeated the same broad contributor fact without adding 2022-specific detail.
- Rejected as too generic: general UN pages on peacekeeping, broad disarmament explainers, and generic charter or development statements that did not add Bangladesh-specific 2022 conduct.
- Rejected as out of period: later 2023-2026 Rohingya and Ukraine retrospectives that did not improve 2022 attribution.
- Rejected as weak for this chapter: sources on humanitarian need or climate vulnerability that did not connect to ruler-attributed international peace choices.
- A few direct opens on mission pages timed out, so I relied on the stable cached text from the search results for those official statements rather than replacing them with weaker secondary summaries.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have direct evidence of Hasina personally making an interstate war decision in 2022, which is expected given Bangladesh’s limited conflict exposure.
- I did not find a 2022 ruler-specific settlement breakthrough on Rohingya repatriation; the record supports diplomatic effort, not success.
- The peacekeeping evidence is strong but institutional, not a direct personal command record.
- More searching would probably only marginally improve attribution on Rohingya diplomacy or on the internal reasoning behind the two Ukraine abstentions; it is unlikely to change the core picture.

